# Handle capability mismatch: @[Handle Writable] annotation on a readable handle
#
# slurp expects Handle[Readable] (a handle with the readable capability flag).
# The second test annotates its result with @[Handle Writable] (a handle with
# only the writable capability flag), which is incompatible. The type checker
# should report a capability mismatch warning.

[
  ---
  # This should type-check cleanly: open returns Handle[Readable Text],
  # and slurp accepts Handle[Readable]. The Text flag is extra but allowed
  # (structural width subtyping — extra capabilities are safe).
  good: [call $slurp [open %cwd "test.txt" Readable Text]]

  ---
  # This should warn: we annotate the handle as @[Handle Writable],
  # but slurp requires Handle[Readable]. The annotation claims the handle
  # has only Writable capability, which is incompatible with Readable.
  bad: [call $slurp [@[Handle Writable] [open %cwd "test.txt" Readable Text]]]
]
=== out
Dict({"good": "[T003]: cannot unify Handle[__cap_flag_readable: Record({})] with Handle[__cap_flag_writable: Record({})]:
=== warn
[T003]: cannot unify Handle[__cap_flag_readable: Record({})] with Handle[__cap_flag_writable: Record({})]:
